{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U6KhzLgrt8BeHXxSq2UuCx","type":"tool_result","content":"# Slice 6b fix round 2 — independent re-review, round 3\n\nReviewed `srvm-phase6b`, HEAD `f650e04650104d225ed6458e689648e1e3d227aa`, against F6/F7, the engineer's fix report, and the continuation design. **F6 and F7 are fixed in the current implementation.** Both requested JVM suites pass. One required production mutant survives both test tiers and demonstrably violates F7's contract; the remaining change request is a narrow regression-test addition, not an established defect in the current implementation.\n\n## Finding\n\n### F8 — Medium: the store census regression does not cover closures inside module-store values\n\n`test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1239`\n\nThe new census test covers result closures, empty stores, frame environments and child/parent isolation. The existing module-store-transitivity test at approximately line 1167 checks **lift-time free-name discharge**, not lower-time store census. Neither detects lower omitting closures nested inside module-store values.\n\nI changed only the closure census input in `check-dependencies!`:\n\n```clojure\n;; mutant: omit slot 7 only while collecting closure rows\n(concat (rows-of (assoc-in task [:slots 7] []) :closure true)\n        (rows-of task :env true))\n```\n\nThis production mutant passes the quick harness (**19 tests, 467 assertions**) and slow harness (**1 test, 698 assertions**), with zero failures/errors. It is not a test-deletion mutant.\n\nAn independent probe establishes the lost contract:\n\n1. Produce a genuine `(fn [] ghost)` closure through the evaluator.\n2. Route the result closure to `outer`, carrying `outer {ghost 1, k <closure routed to inner>}` and `inner {ghost 42}`. The nested closure is owned by the same task and retained only through the outer module store.\n3. Put an authentic stream reference in the root store, making attachment observable.\n4. Lift, decode, remove **only** the `inner` module-store row, and re-emit with `rows/emit`. Leave `outer`, its `k` closure, vectors, trees and all other rows intact.\n5. Lower and invoke `k` from the restored outer module store.\n\n| Carrier / implementation | Lower result | Attaches | Invocation |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Intact / current code | accepts | 1 | 42 |\n| Inner row removed / current code | `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, discovery `:incomplete`, missing stores `#{inner}` | 0 | refused before invocation |\n| Inner row removed / mutant | accepts | 1 | throws `Unable to resolve symbol: ghost in this context`, ex-data `{:symbol ghost}` |\n\nRequired change: add this decoded-and-re-emitted lower regression to the census deftest, including intact transport/invocation and zero-attachment refusal after removing only `inner`. The test must kill the above mutant. No production census change is requested: current `rows-of` correctly reaches this closure.\n\nExecutable probe: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-module-mutant.clj`; baseline and mutant outputs: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-module-baseline.log` and `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-no-module-store-closures-probe.log`.\n\n## Original reproductions\n\nI reran my previous `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-extra.clj`, rather than relying solely on the new tests. Its original F7 intact carrier attaches once and invokes to **42**; stripping all module stores now refuses with **0 attaches**. The original F6 unrelated-store case carries **zero module stores**, attaches once, and both source and receiver complete with **73**.\n\nA separate reproduction repeats F6 both **without** and **with** `unrelated {ghost 42}`: each answers `{:source 73 :receiver 73 :attaches 1}`. The suite also transports the dead-constructor variant `(if false (fn [] ghost) …)` successfully, while reachable reads and reachable closure construction with missing `ghost` refuse.\n\nLogs: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-original.log` and `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-extra.log`.\n\n## Reachability and context assessment\n\n`live-pcs` follows `analysis/successors`, whose return, halt, resume and tail-call instructions terminate control flow. It adds each reachable closure's body entry to the same worklist, so nested closure construction is followed transitively. Closure creation captures the current E in the evaluator; answering a future closure's free occurrences against the creating context is consistent with that behavior. `scan/free-occurrences` removes lexical binders through the enclosure chain.\n\nExisting carried closures do not depend on reaching their constructor again: `closure-node` records an independent entry/E context whenever `lift-value` encounters them. This includes closures in the result, windows, E, free-env, root store and transitively discovered module stores. Module-store discovery finishes before discharge. Separate probes show a nested future closure and a closure retained for later invocation through a captured binding both refuse missing `ghost`; a valid nested future closure has no missing obligation. Existing independent-E and routed-store negatives still pass.\n\nActs and return frames record their resume PCs. Parked-table entries and reified continuations use `state-node`, so they contribute the same contexts and recursively visit their windows and K. A tail state's own dead activation contributes no running context; its K still contributes contexts and carried values. Install children call `lift-task*` with their own machine and accumulator. Their environments/stores cannot answer a parent's or sibling's contexts.\n\nI found no additional false acceptance or false refusal in these supported contexts. The explicit backward-jump unit probe exercises reachability; admitted expression-structured vectors currently forbid backward jumps, so it is not an end-to-end backward-jump transport proof. Branch reachability is conservative over both successor edges, not constant propagation.\n\n## Census coverage and ordering\n\nThe current census reads every closure slot 4 and env slot 1 using `rows-of` with `own-only? true`. The descriptor traversal visits value rows and structural references, including bindings, stores, windows, parked records and reified acts; install children are excluded from this traversal and checked individually by the staged task-tree loop. An install record's parent identifier and response are data, not hidden executable environment rows. Child task rows carry their actual executable state. Missing-store checks execute in dependency preflight before `attach-streams!`.\n\nAdditional canonical re-emission probes route environments specifically beneath `:reified` (corpus row 14), `:parked-table` (row 18), and `:kframe` (row 4) to a carried empty `probe.scope` store. All intact carriers lower successfully; deleting only that store refuses with missing stores `#{probe.scope}` and **0 attaches**. Intact attachment counts are respectively 1, 1 and 2. These probes confirm the descriptor walk covers those locations, beyond the direct frame regression. The existing child regression confirms a parent's same-key store cannot satisfy its child.\n\nProbe and log: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-locations.clj` and `.log`.\n\n## Requested mutations\n\nEach mutation ran separately in a foreground subprocess. Sources were backed up with `cp`/`shutil.copyfile`, restored in `finally`, compared using `diff -q`, and checked with `git diff --quiet -- <target>` **after every mutation**. No source or test edit remains.\n\n| Mutant | Quick harness result | Survivor follow-up |\n|---|---|---|\n| `live-pcs` returns only the resume PC | Killed: **2 failures** | misses future read/closure obligations |\n| Ignore closure body PCs | Killed: **1 failure** | reachable closure obligation |\n| Census ignores env slot 1 | Killed: **5 failures** | frame and child refusals |\n| Census ignores closures inside module stores | **Survives**, 467 assertions | also survives slow, 698 assertions; independent probe admits broken carrier after 1 attach (F8) |\n| Parent census used for children | Killed: **2 failures** | child incorrectly uses parent's same-key store |\n| Drop new corpus attach-count check | **Survives**, 465 assertions | also survives slow, 698 assertions |\n\nThe last mutant removes the two new corpus assertions and changes no production behavior. The per-case `laws` attachment-count assertion and shared-tree assertions remain. Its survival is a test-deletion result, not evidence that attachment counting is vacuous, and is not an additional blocking finding. The module-store-closure mutant, in contrast, changes validation and demonstrably permits attach-before-runtime-failure.\n\nScripts: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-mutants.py`, `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-survivors.py`; logs use `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-mutant-*.log` and the survivor names above.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe public `live-pcs` uses portable vector indexing, sets, sequences and a loop/worklist. It calls the already public portable `analysis/successors`; no private-var quoting, JVM reflection, host numeric class or new host-only exception handling is introduced. Its input contract is admitted code plus a valid context PC, as for the analysis functions it calls. Public exposure permits the ClojureDart regression to invoke it directly; there is no new persisted carrier field or host-specific data. This is source inspection only: **Node and Dart were not run**.\n\n## Verification and final workspace\n\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **51 tests, 1,049 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Same command with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 706 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Logs: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-quick.log`, `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix2-slow.log`.\n- Final byte comparisons against both backups and tracked-file `git diff --quiet` pass. This findings file is the only permanent review addition; no commits, merges or implementation fixes were made.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n1239:(deftest every-store-of-names-a-module-store-of-its-own-task\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1628:(defn- check-dependencies!\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1851:     (doseq [step [check-stores! check-pendings! check-dependencies! check-custody!]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbd25c6b-20a6-4b41-917b-f6d4b9ac1605","uuid":"56f16be9-d11c-4b86-a08f-20e505c66bf9","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:25:02.291Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Slice 6b fix round 2 — independent re-review, round 3\n\nReviewed `srvm-phase6b`, HEAD `f650e04650104d225ed6458e689648e1e3d227aa`, against F6/F7, the engineer's fix report, and the continuation design. **F6 and F7 are fixed in the current implementation.** Both requested JVM suites pass. One required production mutant survives both test tiers and demonstrably violates F7's contract; the remaining change request is a narrow regression-test addition, not an established defect in the current implementation.\n\n## Finding\n\n### F8 — Medium: the store census regression does not cover closures inside module-store values\n\n`test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1239`\n\nThe new census test covers result closures, empty stores, frame environments and child/parent isolation. The existing module-store-transitivity test at approximately line 1167 checks **lift-time free-name discharge**, not lower-time store census. Neither detects lower omitting closures nested inside module-store values.\n\nI changed only the closure census input in `check-dependencies!`:\n\n```clojure\n;; mutant: omit slot 7 only while collecting closure rows\n(concat (rows-of (assoc-in task [:slots 7] []) :closure true)\n        (rows-of task :env true))\n```\n\nThis production mutant passes the quick harness (**19 tests, 467 assertions**) and slow harness (**1 test, 698 assertions**), with zero failures/